Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 22:05:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 075/112] scsi: ufs: qcom: Map 150 MHz UniPro to HS-G3 on
 SM7250

ufs_qcom_freq_to_gear_speed() maps a 150 MHz UniPro clock to HS-G2, which
fits the SoCs with a 300 MHz top level. SM7250's top OPP is 200 MHz core /
150 MHz UniPro (the downstream table tops out there too) and runs HS-G3
at it, so clock scaling never left HS-G2.

With this the link scales to HS-G3 rate B x2 under load: direct reads go
from 434 MB/s to 612 MB/s on a Motorola Moto G 5G Plus, with no UFS
errors through 60 idle/read rounds (clock gating and auto-Hibern8 on).

This patch was generated by an AI coding assistant (Claude Opus 5.5 in
Claude Code) during an interactive bring-up session on a Motorola Moto G
5G Plus (XT2075-3, "nairo"). It has not yet been reviewed line by line
by a human and needs that review before any submission.

Assisted-by: LLM dtschema
---
 drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
index e7e1c004c..9b9720a22 100644
--- a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
@@ -2548,6 +2548,12 @@ static u32 ufs_qcom_freq_to_gear_speed(struct ufs_hba *hba, unsigned long freq)
 		gear = UFS_HS_G3;
 		break;
 	case 150000000:
+		/* SM7250 runs HS-G3 from a 150 MHz UniPro clock */
+		if (of_device_is_compatible(hba->dev->of_node, "qcom,sm7250-ufshc")) {
+			gear = UFS_HS_G3;
+			break;
+		}
+		fallthrough;
 	case 100000000:
 		gear = UFS_HS_G2;
 		break;
